review_comments.body:
**Critical: Clone workers don't stop on first error**

When `concurrency > 1`, a failed `Sandbox.create()` call does not stop the worker or its peers — they continue creating sandboxes until all `n` iterations are exhausted. Every successfully-created sibling is then immediately killed in the error handler (line 647). For example, with `n=10`, `concurrency=3`, if the second attempt fails, all 10 attempts still complete and ~9 sandboxes are created and immediately destroyed.

Add an `AbortController` or `return` guard so that once `firstError` is set, workers skip remaining iterations:

```ts
const aborted = { current: false };
const worker = async () => {
  for (;;) {
    if (aborted.current) return;
    const index = next++;
    if (index >= n) return;
    try { sandboxes.push(await createOne()); }
    catch (err) {
      if (firstError === null) firstError = err;
      aborted.current = true;
    }
  }
};
```

This matches the Python SDK's `ThreadPoolExecutor` which cancels pending futures on failure.

